STEP 0 -- the 4000-range syntax, measured with `?` and no trailing CR       done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4000 deny ?
  MAC  Source host's MAC address in HHHH.HHHH.HHHH format
  any  Source any
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4000 deny 000d.b955.770f ?
  MASK  Source wildcard MAC address in HHHH.HHHH.HHHH format
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4000 deny 000d.b955.770f ffff.ffff.ffff ?
  MAC  Destination host's MAC address in HHHH.HHHH.HHHH format
  any  Destination any
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4000 deny <src> <mask> <dst> <mask> ?
  inner-vlan         inner-vlan
  tunnel-terminated  tunnel-terminated packets
  vlan               vlan
  <cr>
  Form: access-list <4000-4699> <action> <src MAC|any> [<src MASK>] <dst MAC|any> [<dst MASK>]
        [vlan <vid>] [inner-vlan <vid>] [tunnel-terminated]
                                            <-- the parameter set the later steps draw on

STEP 1 -- "a 4000-range MAC list applied to a port denies the matching flow" PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4000 deny 000d.b955.770f 0000.0000.0000 0000.cd42.3029 0000.0000.0000
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group 4000
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 4000
  Hardware MAC access list 4000
      4 deny 000d.b955.770f 0000.0000.0000 0000.cd42.3029 0000.0000.0000
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss        DENIED
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 4000
      Packet Hits: 6                        <-- the rule, not the path, stopped the frames
  => STEP 1 PASS: a numbered hardware MAC ACL bound to a port denies traffic between the
     named source and destination MAC addresses, with the hit counter corroborating.

STEP 2 -- the mask is a WILDCARD, proven from BOTH directions              PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the mask could be read two ways -- 1 = "must match", or 1 = "don't care". A rule
  whose named MAC is the traffic's own MAC matches under EITHER reading, so that alone proves
  nothing. Two independent measurements settle it, and a converse control rules out the
  trivial explanation that masks are ignored altogether.

  (a) VARY THE TRAFFIC, HOLD THE RULE. Frames sent from the spoofed MAC 02:00:00:00:00:99,
      which no rule names:

        list  rule                                                        hits    matched?
        4000  deny 000d.b955.770f 0000.0000.0000 0000.cd42.3029 0000...   0 -> 0  NO
        4002  deny 000d.b955.770f ffff.ffff.ffff any                      0 -> 5  YES

      4002 matched a source MAC it does not name. Under "1 = must match" that is impossible.

  (b) VARY THE RULE, HOLD THE TRAFFIC. A MAC that exists nowhere on this bench, with an
      all-ones mask, against the ordinary 6-ping flow:
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4004 deny 0000.0000.0001 ffff.ffff.ffff any
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 4004
      Packet Hits: 9                        <-- matched traffic from 000d.b955.770f while
                                                naming 0000.0000.0001; under "1 = must match"
                                                this rule would have matched nothing at all

  And the converse control:

        list  rule                                        hits    result
        4001  deny 0000.0000.0001 0000.0000.0000 any      0       0% loss -- matched nothing

      A zero mask against an absent MAC correctly matches nothing, so 0 bits ARE significant
      and the mask is not simply being discarded.

  => STEP 2 PASS: the mask is a WILDCARD -- a 1 bit means "don't care", a 0 bit means "must
     match". Each of the three readings is one of the others' controls, so the conclusion does
     not rest on a single rule.

STEP 3 -- the `any` forms                                                  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4003 deny any any
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 4003
      Packet Hits: 3
  => STEP 3 PASS: `deny any any` denies all traffic ingressing the port, with the counter
     advancing, so the wildcard-any source and destination forms are accepted and effective.

STEP 4 -- the vlan qualifier selects correctly                             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a matching/non-matching pair on rules that are otherwise identical -- same source
  MAC, same destination MAC, same masks -- so the only variable is the VLAN id.

    list  qualifier   .65 result          expected
    4006  vlan 100    100% packet loss    DENY -- the test traffic IS in VLAN 100
    4007  vlan 1        0% packet loss    PASS -- the test traffic is not in VLAN 1

  => STEP 4 PASS: the vlan qualifier narrows a MAC rule to the VLAN it names rather than
     being ignored. Had it been ignored, 4007 would have denied the traffic as 4006 did.

STEP 5 -- copy-to-cpu on a MAC list, BOTH halves proven                    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: copy-to-cpu is defined as "copy to the CPU AND forward". Both halves must be shown;
  proving only one would not distinguish it from send-to-cpu, which does not forward.
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4005 copy-to-cpu 000d.b955.770f 0000.0000.0000 0000.cd42.3029 0000.0000.0000
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group 4005
x960-56YSQ#show platform counter sdma        (read before, then after 8 pings)
              0 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]   ->      9 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]
host# ping -c 8 .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 0% packet loss     <-- STILL FORWARDED
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 4005
      Packet Hits: 9                         <-- equals the CPU queue-0 advance exactly
  => STEP 5 PASS: CPU receive-queue 0 advanced from 0 to 9, exactly matching the 9 ACL hits,
     so the frames were genuinely copied to the CPU; and the ping passed with 0% loss, so they
     were ALSO still forwarded to the far side. That is copy-to-cpu behaving to its
     definition. (Queue 6's counter was also read; it does not accumulate the same way and is
     not used as evidence here.)

STEP 6 -- removal                                                          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Every 4000-range list created in this case was unbound after its own measurement.
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no access-group <n>
  => STEP 6 PASS: each `no access-group <n>` was accepted and the following measurement
     showed the traffic restored, which is also what makes every row above attributable to
     its own list rather than to a leftover binding.
